<main id="main">

<section class="hw-hero"><div class="hw-w hw-hero-g"><div class="hw-hero-t"><p class="hw-k">Pattern No. 01 &middot; cut along the dotted line</p><h1>The right length changes everything.</h1><p class="hw-lead">Hemwork is an independent guide to hems and hemlines. We explain how long trousers, skirts, dresses and sleeves should be, which hem finish suits which fabric, and when a tailor can help.</p><div class="hw-cut"><span>&#9986;</span></div></div><figure class="hw-pin"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1791962831671-c869576205f0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=600&q=75" alt="a tailor in a plaid shirt cutting fabric on a wooden table" width="520" height="600" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Chalk, scissors and a steady hand.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="hw-s" id="break"><div class="hw-w hw-brk"><div class="hw-brk-d"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 360" aria-hidden="true"><path id="lg" d="" fill="#3B4A63"/><path id="fold" d="" fill="#2C384D"/><path d="M60 330h120q24 0 30 18H40q4-18 20-18z" fill="#2A2A2A"/><path d="M92 312h56v20H92z" fill="#4A3427"/><line x1="10" y1="330" x2="210" y2="330" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/></svg></div><div><p class="hw-k">Fitting room</p><h2>How much break?</h2><p>The &ldquo;break&rdquo; is the fold where your trouser hem meets your shoe. Choose one and see the line it makes.</p><div class="hw-opt" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Trouser break"><label><input type="radio" name="bk" value="0"><span>No break</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="bk" value="1" checked><span>Quarter</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="bk" value="2"><span>Half</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="bk" value="3"><span>Full</span></label></div><div class="hw-note" id="bk-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="hw-s hw-grid" id="allow"><div class="hw-w hw-alw"><div><p class="hw-k">Cutting table</p><h2>Hem allowance calculator</h2><p>Planning to shorten something yourself? Enter how much shorter you want it and the hem type. We will tell you where to mark the fold and where to cut.</p><label for="al-t">Garment and hem<select id="al-t"><option value="4">Suit or dress trousers (blind hem, 4 cm)</option><option value="2.5">Jeans (double-fold, 2.5 cm)</option><option value="5">Skirt or dress (blind hem, 5 cm)</option><option value="0.6">Silk or chiffon (rolled hem, 0.6 cm)</option><option value="3">Sleeve (3 cm)</option></select></label><label for="al-s">Shorten by (cm)<input id="al-s" type="number" min="0.5" max="40" step="0.5" value="4"></label></div><div class="hw-paper" id="al-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></section>

<section class="hw-s"><div class="hw-w"><p class="hw-k">Swatch book</p><h2>Six hem finishes</h2><div class="hw-sws"><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 30h200" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-dasharray="2 10" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M0 44h200" stroke="#2A2A2A" stroke-width="1"/></svg><b>Blind hem</b><span>Invisible from the outside. Trousers, skirts, suits.</span></div><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 26h200M0 34h200" stroke="#2A2A2A" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M0 30h200" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-dasharray="8 4" stroke-width="2"/></svg><b>Double-fold</b><span>Folded twice and stitched. Strong and tidy; jeans and casual wear.</span></div><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 30q5-6 10 0t10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0 10 0" fill="none" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-width="2"/></svg><b>Rolled hem</b><span>A tiny rolled edge for light fabrics like silk and chiffon.</span></div><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="0" y="22" width="200" height="16" fill="none" stroke="#2A2A2A"/><path d="M0 30h200" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-dasharray="4 4" stroke-width="2"/></svg><b>Turn-up (cuff)</b><span>Folded to the outside. Adds weight so trousers hang well.</span></div><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 22h200" stroke="#2A2A2A"/><path d="M0 30h200" stroke="#E8C547" stroke-width="5"/><path d="M0 38h200" stroke="#B5322B" stroke-dasharray="6 3" stroke-width="2"/></svg><b>Original hem</b><span>Keeps the factory finish on jeans by moving it up.</span></div><div class="hw-sw"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 30l4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6 4-6 4 6" fill="none" stroke="#2A2A2A"/></svg><b>Raw edge</b><span>Cut and left to fray on purpose. Casual, and it keeps fraying.</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="hw-s hw-ink"><div class="hw-w hw-len"><div class="hw-fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 420" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="100" cy="34" r="22" fill="#F4EBDD"/><path d="M78 60h44l14 110-12 4-8-60v76H84v-76l-8 60-12-4z" fill="#F4EBDD"/><path d="M90 176v200M110 176v200" stroke="#F4EBDD" stroke-width="16" stroke-linecap="round"/><path id="sk" d="M70 168h60l18 0-18 0z" fill="#B5322B"/><g id="ln"></g></svg></div><div><p class="hw-k">Measuring board</p><h2>Skirt and dress lengths</h2><div class="hw-opt" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Hemline"><label><input type="radio" name="hl" value="0"><span>Mini</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="hl" value="1" checked><span>Knee</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="hl" value="2"><span>Midi</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="hl" value="3"><span>Tea</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="hl" value="4"><span>Maxi</span></label></div><div class="hw-note hw-note-d" id="hl-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="hw-s"><div class="hw-w"><p class="hw-k">Ask the tailor</p><h2>Can it be altered?</h2><p>A rough guide to how easy common length alterations are, by garment type.</p><div class="tblw"><table class="hw-tbl"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Job</th><th scope="col">Simple cotton or denim</th><th scope="col">Lined or tailored</th><th scope="col">Detailed (pleats, beads, vents)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Shorten trousers</th><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-e">Easy</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lengthen trousers</th><td class="hw-m">Depends on fold</td><td class="hw-m">Depends</td><td class="hw-h">Rarely</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shorten sleeves</th><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-m">Medium</td><td class="hw-h">Hard (vents)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shorten a skirt</th><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-m">Medium (lining)</td><td class="hw-h">Hard (pleats)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shorten a dress</th><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-m">Medium</td><td class="hw-h">Hard (beading)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Take in the waist</th><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-e">Easy</td><td class="hw-m">Medium</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="hw-row"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792849691024-788694204597?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=500&q=75" alt="a man using a portable sewing machine on a city street" width="400" height="500" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Street repairs, on the spot</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792767658715-f7be37bc6e50?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=500&q=75" alt="a woman using a vintage sewing machine on a sidewalk" width="400" height="500" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A sidewalk machine, still running</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1790956073089-c7221b59ba06?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=500&q=75" alt="an older man threading a needle surrounded by vintage sewing machines" width="400" height="500" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Needles threaded since dawn</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794428483442-06b53b106e82?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=500&q=75" alt="a woman trying on a top while another person adjusts the straps" width="400" height="500" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A final check on the straps</figcaption></figure></section>

<section class="hw-s hw-grid"><div class="hw-w hw-tools"><figure class="hw-pin hw-pin2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794897497867-164b507ab0ec?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=360&q=75" alt="a coiled measuring tape on white fabric pinned to a drafting table" width="360" height="360" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="hw-k">Kit list</p><h2>Six things for neat hems at home</h2><ul class="hw-kit"><li><b>Tape measure</b> soft, in cm and inches</li><li><b>Tailor&rsquo;s chalk</b> marks that brush away</li><li><b>Pins</b> fine, glass-headed ones</li><li><b>Sharp shears</b> used only for fabric</li><li><b>An iron</b> press every fold before sewing</li><li><b>A friend</b> to pin the length while you stand</li></ul></div><figure class="hw-pin hw-pin3"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794897497843-affaddb6e6ad?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=360&q=75" alt="markers, sewing pins and pattern paper on a cutting mat" width="360" height="360" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="hw-s"><div class="hw-w"><p class="hw-k">Pattern envelopes</p><h2>Full guides</h2><div class="hw-env"><a href="trouser-hem-guide.html"><span class="hw-env-n">Pattern 02</span><b>Trouser Hem Guide</b><em>Break, width, turn-ups and how to measure the perfect trouser length.</em><i>Size: all &middot; Difficulty: easy</i></a><a href="hemline-guide.html"><span class="hw-env-n">Pattern 03</span><b>Hemline Guide</b><em>Skirt and dress lengths, proportions and the right finish for each fabric.</em><i>Size: all &middot; Difficulty: easy</i></a></div></div></section>

